+/*
+ * Two small character devices for debugging a running kernel.
+ *
+ * /dev/hlog          a read-only view of the kernel's own message buffer, so the host can watch
+ *                    the log while the phone runs instead of waiting for the run to end and
+ *                    reading it off the flash. The buffer XNU already keeps is the one used --
+ *                    struct msgbuf, exposed as msgbufp -- so nothing is duplicated and the
+ *                    printf() path is untouched.
+ *
+ * /dev/hakios-return  written to, it ends the run and hands the phone back to the loader the
+ *                    same way the run's time expiring does. That is what makes a long
+ *                    loader_return_after comfortable: the run can last ten minutes, and be cut
+ *                    short in a second once the work is done.
+ *
+ * The read side is a plain byte stream with a position, so the reader keeps an offset and asks
+ * for what is new. Reading from offset 0 gives the log from the first line of boot, which is
+ * exactly what the flash-based route could never show while the kernel was still alive.
+ */
+
+
+/* The same path the run's time expiring takes; it does not return. */
